"Number Who Helped Parents Financially Almost as High as Number Who Paid Child Support (https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2023/06/adults-provided-support-to-parents.html)\nciteturn65view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn36search1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 277\nL114: 【39†Census Academy】 【40†Combining Data】 【41†Data Tools and Apps】 【42†Datasets】 【43†Developers】 【44†Experimental Data Products】 【45†Interactive Maps】 【46†Mapping Files】 【47†Profiles】 【48†Related Sites】 【49†Software】 【50†Tables】 【39†Training and Workshops】 【51†Visualizations】\nL115: \nL116: 【4†Survey Help】 【13†View All Surveys & Programs】\nL117: \nL118: * * *\nL119: 【52†2020 Census】 【53†2026 Census Test】 【54†2030 Census】 【16†American Community Survey (ACS)】 【55†American Housing Survey (AHS)】 【56†Annual Business Survey (ABS)】 【57†Annual Integrated Economic Survey (AIES)】 【58†County Business Patterns (CBP)】 【59†Current Population Survey (CPS)】 【60†Economic Census】 【61†Household Pulse Survey】 【62†International Programs】 【63†Metro and Micro Areas】 【64†Population Estimates】 【65†Population Projections】 【66†Small Area Income and Poverty】 【67†Statistics of U.S.\nL120: Businesses】 【68†Survey of Income and Program Participation (SIPP)】\nL121: 【14†View All Library Resources】 【69†Glossary】\nL122: \nL123: * * *\nL124: \nL125: 【70†America Counts: Stories】 【71†Audio】 【72†Blogs】 【73†Facts for Features】 【74†Fact Sheets】 【51†Infographics and Visualizations】 【75†Publications】 【76†Spotlights】 【77†Stats for Stories】 【39†Training (Census Academy)】 【78†Videos】 【79†Working Papers】\nL126: \nL127: 【80†Image】\nL128: ## Number Who Helped Parents Financially Almost as High as Number Who Paid Child Support\nL129: \nL130: June 14, 2023\nL131: \nL132: Written by:\nL133: \nL134: Liza C. Valle\nL135: \nL136: Roughly 4.3 million U.S. adults provided voluntary financial support to parents in 2020 — almost as many as the 4.4 million who made mandatory child support payments.\nL137: But the amount of money spent on parental support was significantly lower: $17.5 billion compared to $30.6 billion for child support, according to the U.S. Census Bureau’s 2021 Survey of Income and Program Participation (SIPP).\nL138: \nL139: In 2020, about 2.4 million U.S. parents received an average of more than $2,000 (median annual payment of $3,749) from adult children.\nL140: \nL141: Money given to parents fell by roughly $9 billion from 2019 to 2020, a year that included the first nine months of the COVID-19 pandemic.\nL142: Estimates from the 2014-2021 SIPP show that over this eight-year period, annual support provided to parents ranged from a low of $10.4 billion in 2014 to a high of $27.9 billion in 2018 (Figure 1).\nL143: \nL144: Since this financial support is voluntary, a fluctuation in annual amounts is expected. However, the drop in support in 2020 may be explained by 【81†financial hardship during the pandemic】, limiting the amount people could give to parents.\nL145: ### Large Share of Payments to Those Living Outside the Household Went to Parents\nL146: \nL147: In 2020, approximately 8.7 million people provided $48.6 billion to support individuals who lived outside their households, including parents and grown children. These providers who voluntarily gave monetary support to people living elsewhere do not include those who gave mandated support in the form of child support or alimony.\nL148: Voluntary support can come in the form of regular or lump sum payments. In 2020, U.S. support providers made voluntary payments to:\nL149: \nL150:   * 6.7 million parents ($17.5 billion).\nL151:   * 3.1 million adult children ages 21 or older who were not away at school ($17.6 billion).\nL152:   * 4.0 million relatives other than parents and adult children ($11.0 billion).\nL153:   * 1.9 million nonrelatives ($2.5 billion).\nL154: Half of all adults who made these payments to people outside their households were helping parents (4.3 million).\nL155: \nL156: In 2020, about 2.4 million U.S. parents received an average of more than $2,000 (median annual payment of $3,749) from adult children.\nL157: \nL158: More than half (53.9%) of parental support providers made payments to one parent and 40.8% to two parents.\nL159: When adult children report supporting only one parent, it is often because that parent is a widow or widower. Of the 2.3 million adult children who reported supporting only one parent, about 1.3 million (or 57.3 %) indicated it was because the other parent was dead.\nL160: Only 5.3% of parental support providers made payments to three or more parents (Table 1), suggesting that a minority of these households were supporting both parents and stepparents. (Payments made to parents-in-law are reported in the SIPP as payments to “other relatives.”)\nL161: ### Who Provided Parental Support?\nL162: \nL163: Those who provided parental support differed demographically from those who did not. They were generally younger (average age 42 vs. 47) and more likely:\nL164: \nL165:   * To be men (57.7% vs. 48.3%).\nL166:   * To be employed (89.7% vs. 62.8%).\nL167:   * To be married (63.2% vs. 48.1%).\nL168:   * To have a bachelor’s degree (39.0% vs. 34.0%).\nL169: Roughly half (45.9%) of those who provided voluntary support to parents living elsewhere were non-citizens. And among those who were U.S. citizens, around 43% were naturalized, suggesting parental giving was more common among immigrants or those who came from immigrant families.\nL170: Among supported parents, 62.4% come from only five countries, with most supported parents having been born in either Mexico (26.0%) or the United States (22.3%). Three of these leading five countries of origin are in Latin America (Mexico, El Salvador, and the Dominican Republic) and accounted for 33.0% of supported parents.\nL171: Additionally, about half (50.2%) of those who provided support to their parents in 2020 were Hispanic. A possible explanation: the parents of 94.3% of Hispanic parental support providers were born in Latin American countries where a considerable share of the older population lacks retirement benefits, according to a 【82†2021 report†www.ilo.org】 from the International Labour Organization, a United Nations agency. So these parents may rely on their children for financial support in their retirement years.\nL172: Around 59.2% of Hispanic parental support providers had at least one parent who was born in Mexico.\nL173: ### About the SIPP\nL174: \nL175: The SIPP is a nationally representative, longitudinal survey administered by the Census Bureau that provides comprehensive information on the dynamics of income, employment, household composition and government program participation.\nL176: More information is available on the 【68†SIPP webpage】. Technical documentation and more information about SIPP data quality are on the 【83†SIPP Technical Documentation webpage】. The estimates presented here are subject to 【84†sampling and nonsampling error】.\nL177: \nL178: Liza C. Valle is an analyst in the Census Bureau’s Social, Economic, and Housing Statistics Division.\nL179: \nL180: This article was filed under:\nL181: \nL182: 【85†Families and Living Arrangements】\nL183: \nL184: 【86†Income】\nL185: \nL186: 【87†Income and Poverty】\nL187: \nL188: 【88†Population】\nL189: ## Related Statistics\nL190: \nL191:   * * * *\nL192: \nL193: 【89†Stats for Stories National Parents’ Day: July 23, 2023 July 23, 2023 Of the 63.2M parents living with their children <18, 76.1% lived with a spouse, 15.1% had no spouse/partner present. The rest lived with a cohabiting partner.】\nL194: \nL195: * * *\nL196: \nL197:   * * * *\nL198: 【90†Stats for Stories National Single Parent Day: March 21, 2024 March 21, 2024 The Current Population Survey (CPS) estimates there were 9.8M one-parent households (7.3M mother only and 2.5M father only) in 2023, compared to 1.5M in 1950.】\nL199: \nL200: * * *\nL201: \nL202:   * * * *\nL203: \nL204: 【91†Stats for Stories Mother’s Day: May 14, 2023 May 14, 2023 The June 2020 Fertility Supplement to the Current Population Survey estimated out of 76M women ages 15 to 50, 41M were mothers who had 89M children ever born.】\nL205: \nL206: * * *\nL207: \nL208:   * * * *\nL209: 【92†Stats for Stories Father’s Day: June 18, 2023 June 18, 2023 Over one in four men — 34.3 million — have a biological child who is under 18. Four out of five of these fathers live with some of those children (79.8%).】\nL210: \nL211: * * *\nL212: ## Subscribe\nL213: \nL214: Our email newsletter is sent out on the day we publish a story. Get an alert directly in your inbox to read, share and blog about our newest stories.\nL215: \nL216: 【93†Sign Up Today†public.govdelivery.com】\nL217: \nL218: Contact our 【94†Public Information Office】 for media inquiries or interviews.\nL219: \nL220: 【95†Related America Counts Stories】\nL221:   * 【96†America Counts Story Over a Quarter of Children Lived With At Least One Foreign-Born Parent February 03, 2022 A new report reveals that children living with at least one foreign-born parent are more likely to live with two parents than children with native-born parents.】\nL222:   * 【97†America Counts Story When Do We Lose Our Parents? May 06, 2019 People lose their fathers earlier in life than their mothers. New Census Bureau data show socio-economic factors also associated with when we lose our parents.】\nL223:   * 【98†America Counts Story Parents and Children Interacted More During COVID-19 January 03, 2022 During Covid-19 lockdowns, parents changed how they interacted with children: more dinners and reading together but fewer outings.】\nL224: ## More Stories\nL225: \nL226: 【70†America Counts: Stories】\nL227:   * 【99†Emergency Preparedness Earthquake Risk May Hit Closer to Home Than You Think October 14, 2026 The 140th anniversary of the Charleston earthquake reminds us of the risks to people, housing and economies in places not typically associated with earthquakes.】\nL228:   * 